  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\n**** Severe Weather Risk This Afternoon ****\n\nA 1012mb low analyzed over western Iowa at 08z/2am will\ngradually deepen as it tracks E/SE across central Illinois\ntoday. Scattered showers are currently ongoing north of the\nsystem...and this trend will continue through midday. As a \nresult, have confined morning PoPs to locations north of I-72.\nThe 00z KILX upper air sounding featured precipitable water of \njust 0.48: however, both the HRRR and RRFS show values\nincreasing into the 0.75-1.00 range ahead of the approaching\nlow. Given the uptick in moisture, REFS mean SBCAPEs increase to\n500-1000J/kg east of the I-55 corridor this afternoon. With\nadequate moisture and strong kinematic fields in play, it\nappears scattered thunderstorms will develop across the E/SE \nKILX CWA later today. While instability increases, storm \nrelative helicity does as well...but does not reach maximum \nvalues of 100-150m2/s2 until just east of the Wabash River late \nin the afternoon. The most recent Day 1 SPC Convective Outlook \nfeatures a 5-15% chance of severe wind/hail along/east of \nI-57...but keeps the 2-5% tornado risk further east along/east \nof the Wabash River in line with where the strongest \ninstability/helicity will likely coexist. Think a few gusty \nthunderstorms capable of producing marginally severe winds will\ndevelop across the E/SE between 1pm and 5pm...then quickly push\neastward into Indiana toward sunset.\n\n\n**** End of Week Cool-Down ****\n\nOnce the low tracks into the Ohio River Valley, winds will veer\nto northerly and temperatures will begin to fall Wednesday night\nas overnight lows drop into the upper 30s and lower 40s. As a\nshort-wave trough noted on the latest water vapor imagery over\nnorthern Manitoba/Ontario dives southward and re-enforces the\nlong-wave trough east of the Mississippi River, it will provide\nsufficient lift to trigger scattered light showers on Thursday.\nThanks to ample cloud cover and strong CAA, high temperatures\nwill remain in the middle to upper 40s across much of the\nCWA...with lower 50s confined to locations along/south of I-70.\nThe current forecast features northerly wind gusts of 20-25mph:\nhowever, the 00z NBM hints that even higher gusts will be\npossible...with a 20-30% chance of gusts greater than 30mph. The\nstrong winds will make it feel even cooler, as wind-chill \nvalues hover in the 30s for much of the day.\n\nWill still need to keep an eye on a band of lake-enhanced\nshowers from Lake Michigan: however, the boundary layer fetch\nshould generally keep it E/NE of central Illinois across\nnortheast Illinois into north-central Indiana Thursday night\ninto Friday. Despite a return to partial sunshine, Friday will\nbe the coolest day of the forecast period as highs only reach\nthe lower to middle 40s. As high pressure builds into the\nregion, skies will clear and winds will become nearly calm over\nthe weekend. The end result will be cool days with highs in the\n40s and overnight lows in the 20s. After that, the dominant\nupper trough will gradually shift E/NE away from the region and\nheights will rise next week. This will allow temperatures to\nmoderate back to near or slightly above normal in the middle to\nupper 50s by next Wednesday.",
